{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Cut the overlap step's token cost: hoist the shared query digest into the cacheable system prefix\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`rebar review-plan` spends the majority of its input tokens in the Stage-2 overlap judge. Measured\non this repo against the real API after bug `afeb` landed (so `overlap-judge` already runs on the\n`standard` class, `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`): 12 judge calls over a 6-candidate corpus of real\n`rebar.llm.enrich` digests billed **16,040 input tokens**, with `cache_read_tokens: 0` and\n`cache_write_tokens: 0` on every call. A recording proxy runner wrapped around the real runner in\n`overlap.judge.judge()` showed the query ticket's digest block present verbatim in the user message\nof **12 of 12** calls, while the system prompt was byte-identical across all 12. The bytes that are\nidentical on every call are paid at full price every time, because they sit in the volatile user\nblock, which is never a cache prefix.\n\n## Approach\n\nHoist the one digest that is invariant across a batch — the query ticket's — out of the per-call\nuser message and into the system prompt, so the shared bytes sit inside the block that already\ncarries a cache breakpoint. The engine already applies `anthropic_cache_instructions=True` to the\nsystem block at the shared runner seam (`llm/capabilities.py:cache_settings_for`, dispatched from\n`llm/structured_run.py`), so no new caching machinery is added; the only reason today's calls cache\nnothing is that the stable block is too small. Concretely: `judge_one` gains one keyword-only\nargument naming which side of the pair is the hoisted digest, a module-private helper appends the\ndigest to the system prompt, and another substitutes a fixed reference string into the slot the\nhoisted digest vacated.\n\nExact wording, so nothing load-bearing is left to the implementer's discretion. The system prompt\nbecomes the base prompt followed by exactly:\n\n    QUERY TICKET DIGEST — one of the two digests named below is given HERE rather than in the\n    user message, because it is the same ticket in every pair of this batch. The user message\n    names whether it is FIRST or SECOND for the pair at hand; read it in that position.\n\n    <the four-line digest block produced by _digest_block>\n\nand the reference substituted into the vacated slot is exactly\n`the QUERY TICKET DIGEST given in your system prompt`. That orientation paragraph also reconciles\nthe base prompt's framing (`reviewers/overlap_judge.md`: \"You are given TWO ticket digests in a\nfixed order\"), which otherwise implies both digests arrive inline in the user message. It is\nappended by `judge.py` rather than edited into `overlap_judge.md`, so the prompt file keeps working\nunchanged for the default (non-hoisted) call shape.\n\n`judge()` judges each candidate in BOTH orderings — `(query, cand)` and `(cand, query)` — so the\nquery digest is FIRST in one and SECOND in the other; passing `\"first\"` and `\"second\"` respectively\npreserves the directional reading the prompt depends on. Because the hoisted content is the same\nquery digest either way, the system block is byte-identical across all 2N calls of a batch: one\ncache write on the first call, reads on the remaining 2N-1, whenever the prefix is large enough to\ncache at all.\n\n**This is NOT a byte-for-byte relocation, and it is NOT assumed to be behaviour-preserving.** The\nuser block genuinely changes for the query side: an inline digest is replaced by a pointer to\nanother block, which is a semantic change to a prompt whose own instructions say \"false flags are\ncostly\". The engine's `resolve_prompt_cached` precedent (`llm/prompting/prompts.py:438`) is a pure\nstable/volatile split that does not change what the model reads, so it underwrites the SHAPE of the\nchange but not its harmlessness. Behavioural equivalence is therefore a thing this ticket MEASURES,\nnot a thing it asserts: a verdict-parity run over the same corpus (below) compares the per-pair\n`relation`, `shared_artifact` and `confidence` produced by the inline-digest baseline against the\nhoisted build, and a divergence is a finding to report, not a rounding error to absorb.\n\n**Riskiest assumption, tested first — and it has already been measured, in the unfavourable\ndirection.** The design pays off only if the hoisted prefix reaches the model's minimum cacheable\nprefix. `llm/capabilities.py:330` hardcodes `CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS = 4096` and documents it as the\n*Opus 4.8* floor. Two probes settled what actually applies to `claude-sonnet-4-6`:\n\n| probe | system tokens (Anthropic `count_tokens`) | call 1 write/read | call 2 write/read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| bare API, `cache_control` on system | 922 | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| bare API | 1042 | 1035 / 0 | 0 / 1035 |\n| through the real judge request path | 875 | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| through the real judge request path | 1250 | 1243 / 0 | 0 / 1243 |\n\nSo the real floor is **1024**, not 4096, and the caching machinery demonstrably works on this exact\nrequest path. But the hoisted prefix does not reach it: `count_tokens` over the base prompt plus each\nof the seven real digests gives **728–875 tokens** (base 423; digests 306–453) — every one short of\n1024 by 149–296 tokens. A scratch run of the hoisted payload against the real API over the same\n12 calls confirmed the consequence directly: `cache_read_tokens: 0`, `cache_write_tokens: 0`, and\n**16,748** input tokens versus the baseline's 16,040 — a **+708 token (+4.4%) increase**, entirely\nattributable to the added orientation paragraph.\n\n**Why land it anyway, stated plainly rather than dressed up as a win.** The hoist is the structural\nprecondition for any caching of this call site: while the shared digest sits in the user block, no\nfloor, model change, or prompt growth can ever make it cacheable, and the cost question stays\nunanswerable. With the digest in the stable block the entire question collapses to one measurable\nnumber — stable-prefix tokens versus the model's floor — which this ticket pins at 728–875 vs 1024\nand hands to the epic as a decided fact instead of an estimate. The residual cost is bounded and\nsmall (one orientation paragraph, ~50 tokens per call), and the criteria below require it to be\nreported honestly as a regression if that is what the final measurement shows. The alternative\nlever for closing the 149–296 token gap (batching several candidates into one call, which cuts call\ncount AND raises the prefix past the floor) is deliberately NOT taken here — it changes the judge's\nprecision contract and belongs on its own ticket, filed as discovered work with these measurements\nattached.\n\nDeliberately NOT done here: the loop is not parallelized. `judge()` is a sequential\n`for cand in candidates:`, so the first call warms the prefix and the rest read it. Fanning out cold\ncalls that share a prefix would turn one cache write plus N-1 cheap reads into N premium writes —\nstrictly worse than today — so parallelism stays out until it can be sequenced behind a completed\nwarm-up call.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/overlap/judge.py` — the only production change: the keyword-only shared-side\n  argument on `judge_one`, the two module-private helpers, and the two call sites in `judge()`.\n  Omitting the argument reproduces today's exact request bytes.\n- `tests/unit/test_overlap_judge.py` — the new tests below, added to the existing file.\n\nNo other file changes. `src/rebar/llm/overlap/retrieve.py` and `src/rebar/llm/overlap/wire.py` were\ndeclared while the BM25F-tightening and fail-safe options were still open; the chosen design touches\nneither, and both fail-safes (`wire.overlap_findings`'s `except` and `judge_one`'s `except`) are\npreserved unedited. `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/overlap_judge.md` is deliberately NOT edited, for the\nreason given above. `llm/capabilities.py`'s 4096 constant is NOT changed here: it is correct for the\nOpus-class model it documents, and making it model-aware is separate work filed as discovered.\n\n## Testing\n\nOffline tests drive the real `judge()` through a recording fake runner that captures each\n`RunRequest`, so they assert on the ACTUAL request payload, never on a proxy that would pass either\nway. The behavioural and token questions are settled by real-API runs recorded on the ticket.\n\n- **Happy path — the shared digest is hoisted and not duplicated.** One query digest, two candidate\n  digests (4 calls). Assert every captured `system_prompt` is byte-identical; the query digest block\n  appears in it; the query digest block appears in ZERO of the 4 `instructions`; each candidate's\n  block appears in its own `instructions`.\n- **Ordered-pair semantics survive the hoist.** For the same run, assert the reference string\n  occupies the FIRST slot and the candidate block the SECOND slot in ordering 1, and the reverse in\n  ordering 2.\n- **Failure/edge path.** A runner raising on every call still yields `judge(...) == []` with nothing\n  propagating; a candidate absent from the corpus is skipped with no call made.\n- **Back-compat edge.** `judge_one` without the new argument produces `system_prompt` and\n  `instructions` byte-identical to today's shape.\n- **Behavioural equivalence, real API.** The same query ticket and the same candidate corpus are run\n  twice — once on the inline-digest baseline, once on the hoisted build — and the per-pair\n  `relation`, `shared_artifact` and `confidence` are compared pair by pair.\n- **Mutation check, both directions, for every new test:** revert `judge.py`, confirm each new test\n  FAILS; restore, confirm it passes. Recorded on the ticket.\n- Gates: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 6\n  --dist worksteal -q`, judged by exit code.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The query digest is sent in the system prompt and nowhere else: a recording-runner test over\n      `judge()` shows it in a byte-identical `system_prompt` on every call and in ZERO\n      `instructions`; checked by the new test in `tests/unit/test_overlap_judge.py`.\n- [ ] Ordered-pair semantics are preserved in the payload: a test asserts the reference string is in\n      the FIRST slot for ordering 1 and the SECOND slot for ordering 2, candidate block in the\n      other; checked by the new ordering test.\n- [ ] Judgement precision is shown, not assumed: a real-API verdict-parity run over one query ticket\n      and at least six candidate digests reports, per ordered pair, the baseline vs hoisted\n      `relation` / `shared_artifact` / `confidence`, and is recorded as a ticket comment. Any pair\n      whose `relation` differs is called out explicitly with its digests; identical relations on\n      every pair is the pass condition, and a divergence must be reported rather than averaged away.\n- [ ] The step stays advisory and fail-safe: with a runner that raises on every call, `judge()`\n      returns `[]` and raises nothing; checked by the extended error test.\n- [ ] The default path is byte-identical: `judge_one` without the new keyword argument produces the\n      same `system_prompt` and `instructions` as before; checked by the back-compat test.\n- [ ] A BEFORE/AFTER measurement from real `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` calls over the SAME query\n      ticket and SAME corpus is recorded as a ticket comment reporting total `input_tokens`,\n      `cache_read_tokens` AND `cache_write_tokens` for both runs, plus the `count_tokens` size of the\n      hoisted prefix beside the measured 1024 floor. `cache_write_tokens` above one prefix write\n      FAILS this criterion. A net token INCREASE is a valid outcome and must be recorded as a\n      regression in plain terms, not presented as a win.\n- [ ] The measured shortfall is handed on rather than dropped: a follow-up ticket is created and\n      linked `discovered_from` this one, carrying the 728–875 vs 1024 numbers and naming the\n      call-batching lever as the way to close the gap; checked by the linked ticket id being\n      recorded in a comment here.\n- [ ] Every new test is mutation-checked in both directions (fails with `judge.py` reverted, passes\n      restored) and the evidence recorded as a ticket comment.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the non-integration pytest run all exit 0."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785709809326167001,"uuid":"213b0198-90f1-4518-8a7b-49091e707b8a"}